    <TIER LINGUISTIC_TYPE_REF="default-lt" TIER_ID="default"/>
    <TIER LINGUISTIC_TYPE_REF="imported-sep" TIER_ID="SPEAKER_00">
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a1"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts1" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts5">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Today, we're talking about positive communication skills. We have a very special guest, Dr. Julien Mirivel. So, let's get into the details.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a2"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts8" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts25">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Hey there, welcome back. Alex Lyon here. And today we have a very special guest, Dr. Julian Miravelle. He's professor and interim dean at the University of Arkansas Little Rock, he's here to talk to us today about positive communication skills. And you could say, he wrote the book on it. "The Art of Positive Communication". It's available on Amazon. It's a real pleasure to have him here talking to us today. And he's also a really good friend of mine. So please welcome Dr. Julien Mirivel. How are you doing, my old friend?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a7"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts28" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts34">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Thanks for being here. I really appreciate it. I'm glad we finally got you on the channel. So, let's jump right in. How did you first become interested in positive communication and how did this model come about?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a9"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts38" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts44">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Wonderful. And this model. Has six part see, six key communication skills or what you call critical behaviors. So let's go through all six for our viewers. The first one is the importance of greeting people. What is so important about greeting one another?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a13"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts50" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts52">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah, I've noticed that myself. If I'm meeting up with somebody or if I bump into somebody, if the greeting is a little off, if it doesn't have that positive connection, it really sets the strange tone for the rest of the conversation.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a15"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts58" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts62">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Excellent. All right, so start with a good, strong greeting, a warm greeting, a greeting that presses in and connects with the other people. So, behavior number two is about asking good questions. So, what kinds of questions really build relationships?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a17"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts64" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts68">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>So those open-ended questions might start with how or what and it invites people to share more detail and literally open the conversation rather than the closed-ended question sort of shutting that question down really quickly and not really inviting much detail. A yes or no question doesn't invite much detail. It doesn't lead to those warm positive relationship building moments.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a19"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts74" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts76">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Excellent. And number three, another aspect of positive communication is helping people to feel good about themselves. So how can our viewers make other people feel good about themselves?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a21"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts82" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts84">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>That's wonderful. So along the same lines, how can our viewers create more closeness with other people?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a23"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts90" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts95">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah, and I got to say, you practice what you preach when you send me texts, you say, hey, I've been thinking about you, and you really do that well. And what it does is it frees me up to reciprocate. It creates room for me to be authentic in return. And then you do feel closer. You know, I can't tell you how many times you've said, hey, man, just thinking about you, miss you, looking forward to talking soon. And if I'm like hanging out with my wife, Erin, I'll say, oh, Julien was just saying this nice thing isn't that great and it really does create that bond of closeness so i do appreciate that and you you're not just writing about [overlap] it in a book you've lived it</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a128"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts96" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts99">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah, sure</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a131"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts102" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts106">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>[laugh]</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a26"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts108" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts112">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Right. Yeah, there's a little risk involved in expressing closeness, but more I find more often than not, if you're around the right people to begin with, they do appreciate that and they handle it pretty well. They may not know how to do it back yet, but you're leading by example, by trying. So number five in the model is about when people are not so we're not always feeling great. Sometimes we're struggling, right? So what's the best way to communicate when people are feeling down?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a31"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts122" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts124">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah, I like how you emphasize this. It can be almost anything. You don't have to say the perfect statement or the thing that is so deep and so profound. It can just be your way. Almost anything you say to say you're there for them shows them that you are in it with them, that if they want to say more, just knowing you're available for that is a huge encouragement to them. You don't have to solve their problem with the perfect expression. Just any, just trying to begin with is a great move in the right direction when people are down. So number six, we can't get away from this without talking about listening. It's a huge part of good communication. So what are some easy ways that we can overcome those common listening barriers?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a132"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts130" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts133">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a35"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts136" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts138">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah. And I really like the opening up. The physical gesture of opening your hands is more than just a physical gesture. It is, it puts you in the state of mind of being open. And also, I like how you said you don't have to agree. Being willing to listen does not mean you're agreeing with the person. You're just becoming a better listener. And you might just learn something. You may not agree, but at least you appreciate where they're coming from at a deeper level and they're going to feel more heard. And that creates a bond as well, even over a difficult conversation. They feel respected. They feel valued when you really listen.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a40"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts148" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts152">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Awesome. Thank you so much, Julien. So let's tell people again, they can get your book on Amazon, The Art of Positive Communication. They can find your website at julienmirivel.com and your TEDx talk is on YouTube. I will put links to all of those resources in the description below this video. Julien, thank you so much, my friend, for being with us today. It was a real treat.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a42"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts157" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts158">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Thanks. And if this is the first time you're watching this channel, Communication Coach, we have a lot more videos. So feel free to check out the channel. Take care.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
    </TIER>
